WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.841 --> 00:00:05.741
The first test level: the partner tests its own configuration, the system test.

2
00:00:06.818 --> 00:00:11.473
Then the chain test: a whole process across all systems, order to invoice.

3
00:00:12.510 --> 00:00:13.427
Then the UAT,

4
00:00:13.613 --> 00:00:18.733
the user acceptance test: future users check whether the system supports their work,

5
00:00:18.873 --> 00:00:19.790
and accept it.

6
00:00:20.845 --> 00:00:24.816
ISTQB, the international board for software testing,

7
00:00:24.955 --> 00:00:28.960
described acceptance testing as formal testing against user needs,

8
00:00:29.112 --> 00:00:31.306
requirements and business processes.

9
00:00:32.607 --> 00:00:36.392
Key users test: experienced people from every department,

10
00:00:36.554 --> 00:00:39.712
who help design the system and later train colleagues.

11
00:00:40.441 --> 00:00:41.370
Not the partner.

12
00:00:42.380 --> 00:00:47.488
They test with test scenarios from real work: a rush order during a shortage,

13
00:00:47.663 --> 00:00:50.600
a blocked half-packed batch, a returned pallet.

14
00:00:51.662 --> 00:00:56.154
Each scenario links back to the requirements, so every Must is tested.

15
00:00:57.484 --> 00:01:02.767
A test script spells a scenario out: steps, input and expected result.

16
00:01:03.555 --> 00:01:05.982
The tester notes what happened: pass or fail.

17
00:01:07.008 --> 00:01:09.864
Use real data from the last mock migration:

18
00:01:10.015 --> 00:01:13.939
a key user spots a familiar article with the wrong unit at once,

19
00:01:14.067 --> 00:01:15.599
or a routing missing a step.

20
00:01:16.663 --> 00:01:20.320
With invented data, those errors stay hidden until go-live.

21
00:01:21.648 --> 00:01:23.517
The UAT ends with sign-off:

22
00:01:23.750 --> 00:01:27.871
the process owners formally accept that it met the agreed criteria,

23
00:01:28.011 --> 00:01:29.950
often tied to a partner payment.

24
00:01:31.019 --> 00:01:33.992
It is not the training, though it helps key users learn,

25
00:01:34.177 --> 00:01:37.857
nor the moment for new wishes: those go to the change process.

26
00:01:38.871 --> 00:01:43.364
Often the build runs late, the UAT is squeezed from four weeks to one,

27
00:01:43.596 --> 00:01:46.475
and key users test between their normal work.

28
00:01:47.516 --> 00:01:51.197
Or they test only the happy path, where everything goes right.

29
00:01:52.251 --> 00:01:56.617
Ask: who tests, how many hours a week are they freed, and with which data?

30
00:01:57.942 --> 00:02:02.551
A food producer plans the UAT for its new ERP: four processes,

31
00:02:02.783 --> 00:02:06.824
a hundred and twenty test scripts, awkward cases included.

32
00:02:07.886 --> 00:02:10.812
One script takes an hour and a half on average,

33
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:15.271
including preparing the data: a hundred and eighty hours for round one.

34
00:02:16.276 --> 00:02:17.286
Eight key users,

35
00:02:17.542 --> 00:02:20.177
each freed for half of a thirty-six-hour week:

36
00:02:20.351 --> 00:02:22.801
a hundred and forty-four hours a week.

37
00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:26.785
So one round takes one point two five weeks.

38
00:02:27.781 --> 00:02:30.893
Yet the project lead plans two weeks for round one,

39
00:02:31.031 --> 00:02:35.280
with room for defects and waiting, and one week to retest what failed.

40
00:02:36.357 --> 00:02:40.421
Backfill for the key users is arranged with the shift leaders beforehand.

41
00:02:41.799 --> 00:02:46.188
A defect is a result that differs from the expected one, logged with steps,

42
00:02:46.315 --> 00:02:49.496
screenshot and script ID, so others can repeat it.

43
00:02:50.584 --> 00:02:54.531
Not every defect is a system fault: it can be wrong test data,

44
00:02:54.647 --> 00:02:57.968
something the tester has not learned yet, or a new wish.

45
00:02:58.966 --> 00:03:00.754
The severity is the impact.

46
00:03:01.523 --> 00:03:05.030
One: blocking, the process cannot continue, such as shipping.

47
00:03:05.759 --> 00:03:08.058
Two: serious, with a workaround.

48
00:03:08.842 --> 00:03:09.794
Three: minor.

49
00:03:10.449 --> 00:03:12.527
Four: cosmetic, such as a typo.

50
00:03:13.521 --> 00:03:18.723
Priority is how fast to fix it: usually as severity, but a date can raise it,

51
00:03:18.908 --> 00:03:22.077
like a label format for a customer launching next month.

52
00:03:23.331 --> 00:03:25.920
Triage is the daily meeting where test lead,

53
00:03:26.036 --> 00:03:30.471
partner, key users and a process owner sort new defects: a real fault?

54
00:03:31.221 --> 00:03:31.918
How severe?

55
00:03:32.634 --> 00:03:34.004
Who fixes it, by when?

56
00:03:35.031 --> 00:03:39.780
For a Lean consultant it resembles an andon response: quick, close to the work,

57
00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:41.347
with the people who know it.

58
00:03:42.176 --> 00:03:46.229
The process owner has the last word on severity, not the partner.

59
00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:50.764
After a fix, the tester reruns the script: a retest.

60
00:03:51.531 --> 00:03:54.143
If it passes, the defect is closed.

61
00:03:55.170 --> 00:03:57.179
A fix can break something elsewhere,

62
00:03:57.341 --> 00:04:00.650
so a regression test reruns scripts that passed earlier.

63
00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:03.533
The bigger the fix, the bigger the set.

64
00:04:04.868 --> 00:04:07.515
The UAT ends on exit criteria:

65
00:04:07.689 --> 00:04:11.068
conditions agreed in advance that say when it is finished.

66
00:04:12.075 --> 00:04:16.394
For example: all scripts run, at least ninety-five percent passed,

67
00:04:16.557 --> 00:04:22.036
no open severity one, severity two only with an accepted workaround and a fix date.

68
00:04:23.114 --> 00:04:27.340
Ask early: who decides severity, and can the partner lower it alone?

69
00:04:28.063 --> 00:04:32.010
If so, the criteria are only as strong as the partner wants.

70
00:04:33.305 --> 00:04:37.531
Day eight of the UAT for a new MES at a plastics processor.

71
00:04:38.276 --> 00:04:42.131
Of a hundred and twenty scripts, a hundred and twelve have run,

72
00:04:42.328 --> 00:04:45.997
a hundred and one passed; twenty-three defects are open.

73
00:04:47.008 --> 00:04:51.838
Triage finds six are no fault: three wrong test data, two a training gap,

74
00:04:52.036 --> 00:04:52.965
one a new wish.

75
00:04:53.996 --> 00:04:59.209
Seventeen remain: two of severity one, five of two, seven of three, three of four.

76
00:05:00.279 --> 00:05:05.108
Those two: a work order cannot be closed, and the label misses the batch number.

77
00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:10.539
The exit criteria ask ninety-five percent of a hundred and twenty:

78
00:05:10.713 --> 00:05:12.164
a hundred and fourteen.

79
00:05:12.885 --> 00:05:14.661
So thirteen more must pass.

80
00:05:15.673 --> 00:05:20.502
Both severity ones must be fixed, retested and covered by a regression set.

81
00:05:21.258 --> 00:05:23.661
The partner gets them first, with a date.
